//? Title: Finite Elements: Nodal Values, Whole Fields and Actual Time Levels
//? Subtitle: P1 weak forms, boundary signs, manufactured solutions and a checked transient source path
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Solve steady and transient boundary-value problems with the native one- and
two-dimensional P1 finite-element APIs. A two-element interval shows how a
single free coefficient is determined, and why exact values at mesh nodes
can coexist with error between those nodes. We retain the original diffusion,
reaction, Neumann, Robin, transient and triangular Poisson examples. Actual
refinement errors and time slices are computed in complete C++ programs.
The named-source transient entry point shares the steady source definitions
and the canonical backward-Euler loop. A rounded final-time example makes a
corrected duplicate-time failure visible. Norm claims, stability assumptions,
quadrature and boundary signs are kept distinct from measured outcomes.
)

\toc

> Two elements determine one coefficient

Solve −u''=1 on [0,1] with zero endpoint values. On the mesh [0,1/2,1],
the only free coefficient is the midpoint value. Each length-1/2 element
contributes 1/h=2 to its diagonal stiffness and h/2=1/4 to its load.
The assembled free equation is therefore 4u_1=1/2, giving u_1=1/8.
The program obtains that value from the native solve and checks the tiny equation.

The exact solution is x(1−x)/2, which agrees at all three nodes. The P1 field
is a straight line on each element, not a quadratic. At x=1/4, native linear
interpolation gives 1/16, while the exact quadratic is 3/32: the error is
1/32. This distinction is central to interpreting every later error table.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fem_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/fem/fem.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/interpolation_extra.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/cubic_spline.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <limits>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <vector>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto mesh=dm::fem::make_uniform_mesh1d(0,1,2);const auto result=dm::fem::FEM1D::solve_builtin(mesh,{1},{0},"constant",{1},{},{});
  std::print("| node | coordinate | actual FEM value | exact quadratic |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");for(std::size_t i=0;i<result.size();++i){const auto x=result.nodes[i];std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i,x,result.u[i],x*(1-x)/2);}
  const double p1=dm::algorithms::linear_interpolate(result.nodes,result.u,.25),exact=.25*.75/2;std::print("\nTiny free equation: 4*u_mid = {}; actual u_mid {}.\n",4*result.u[1],result.u[1]);std::print("Between nodes at x=0.25: P1 {}; exact {}; absolute error {}.\n",p1,exact,std::abs(p1-exact));
  dm::graphics::Diagram g(58,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=3.2;box.height=1.5;box.font_size=23;box.fill={226,232,240};g.node("left",{0,0},"x=0\nDirichlet u=0",box);box.fill={187,247,208};g.node("middle",{5,0},std::format("x=1/2\nu={}",result.u[1]),box);box.fill={226,232,240};g.node("right",{10,0},"x=1\nDirichlet u=0",box).edge("left","middle","element 0").edge("right","middle","element 1");g.text({5,2.7},"Two elements contribute 2 + 2 stiffness and 1/4 + 1/4 load",25).text({5,-2.2},"One free equation: 4 u(1/2) = 1/2",26);
  dm::graphics::DiagramStroke stroke;stroke.color={37,99,235};stroke.width=3;g.path({{0,-6},{5,-3.4},{10,-6}},stroke).text({5,-7.6},"P1 field = 0.125 × the midpoint hat function",25).text({5,-9.2},"Nodal exactness leaves between-node error: 1/32 at x=1/4",23).save_svg("build/fem_tiny.svg");if(result.u[1]!=.125||std::abs(p1-exact)!=.03125)throw std::runtime_error("tiny FEM identity failed");
}
