\caption(The solved coefficient is one eighth. The native hat schematic shows the P1 representation, while the printed quarter-point check demonstrates a nonzero error between exact mesh nodes.)
\alttext(A native three-node interval diagram shows zero endpoint values and midpoint coefficient one eighth, the assembled equation four times the midpoint value equals one half, and a triangular midpoint hat. A label identifies quarter-point error one thirty-second.)

> From the weak form to the native interval system

For −(p u')'+q u=f, multiply by a test function v that vanishes at Dirichlet
endpoints and integrate by parts. The resulting equation is

\[
\int p u'v'\,dx+\int q uv\,dx
=\int fv\,dx+\sum_{\text{endpoints}}p\,\frac{du}{dn}v.
\]

Choose continuous piecewise-linear trial and test functions on the same mesh.
With u_h=sum_j U_j phi_j, the coefficient equation is KU=F. Each interval of
length h and constant p_e,q_e contributes

\[
\frac{p_e}{h}\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\-1&1\end{pmatrix}
+\frac{q_eh}{6}\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}.
\]

Only neighboring hats overlap, giving a tridiagonal system. The implementation
uses symmetric Dirichlet elimination and the Thomas direct solver. It requires
valid finite increasing mesh nodes, compatible coefficient lengths and a finite,
nonsingular system with nonzero elimination pivots; it does not supply a pivoting
or conditioning certificate. Constant/per-element p and q are supported.

The interval load uses three-point Gauss–Legendre quadrature, exact through
degree five of the integrand. Since the integrand includes a linear basis
function, this exactly integrates polynomial sources through degree four on
an element. Smooth sine loads are quadrature approximations. These choices
are part of the native method, not extra report-local assembly code.

> Retain the original interval problems and measure the right error

The original pure-diffusion meshes contain 2,5,20 elements. Their quadratic
solution is nodally exact to rounding, while its piecewise-linear interpolant
still differs between nodes. The first plot uses the two-element case.
For that quadratic, the maximum interpolation error on an element is h²/8.

The original reaction problem is −u''+u=(pi²+1)sin(pi x) with zero endpoint
values, so its manufactured exact solution is sin(pi x). The refinement table
uses the same 4,8,16,32,64,128 element counts. Native error scans evaluate the
actual P1 field and reference at mesh nodes; they are sampled maxima, not
certified suprema over the interval. The slope-two guide is normalized to
the first measured error and is a visual comparison, not a new error bound.

For regular/coercive problems and suitable meshes, standard P1 estimates give
first-order H1 and second-order L2 error under the required solution and dual
regularity. The L2 duality argument does not automatically establish a generic
pointwise or nodal second-order bound. The rates below describe this particular
smooth problem, mesh sequence, quadrature and solver accuracy.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fem_interval.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/fem/fem.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/interpolation_extra.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/cubic_spline.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <limits>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <vector>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::print("| pure-diffusion elements | actual maximum nodal error |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");for(std::size_t n:{2,5,20}){const auto r=dm::fem::FEM1D::solve_builtin(dm::fem::make_uniform_mesh1d(0,1,n),{1},{0},"constant",{1},{},{});const auto e=dm::algorithms::interpolation_error_scan([&](double x){return dm::algorithms::linear_interpolate(r.nodes,r.u,x);},[](double x){return x*(1-x)/2;},r.nodes);std::print("| {} | {:.10e} |\n",n,e.max_abs_error);}
  const auto tiny=dm::fem::FEM1D::solve_builtin(dm::fem::make_uniform_mesh1d(0,1,2),{1},{0},"constant",{1},{},{});std::vector<double>x,truth;for(int i=0;i<=200;++i){const double z=i/200.;x.push_back(z);truth.push_back(z*(1-z)/2);}auto field=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();field.line(x,truth,"Exact quadratic",{37,99,235},2).line(tiny.nodes,tiny.u,"P1, two elements",{220,38,38},2).points(tiny.nodes,tiny.u,"Actual nodes",{220,38,38},5).title("Exact nodes, different whole fields").x_label("x").y_label("u(x)");
  const double pi=3.14159265358979323846;std::vector<double>lh,le,guide;double previous=0,first=0;std::print("\n| reaction elements | h | actual maximum nodal error | observed order |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");for(std::size_t n:{4,8,16,32,64,128}){const auto r=dm::fem::FEM1D::solve_builtin(dm::fem::make_uniform_mesh1d(0,1,n),{1},{1},"sine",{pi*pi+1,pi,0},{},{});const auto e=dm::algorithms::interpolation_error_scan([&](double z){return dm::algorithms::linear_interpolate(r.nodes,r.u,z);},[=](double z){return std::sin(pi*z);},r.nodes);const double h=1./n;std::print("| {} | {:.8f} | {:.10e} | {} |\n",n,h,e.max_abs_error,previous?std::format("{:.6f}",std::log2(previous/e.max_abs_error)):"initial");if(!first)first=e.max_abs_error;lh.push_back(std::log10(h));le.push_back(std::log10(e.max_abs_error));guide.push_back(std::log10(first*std::pow(h/.25,2)));previous=e.max_abs_error;}
  auto convergence=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();convergence.line(lh,le,"Measured error",{124,58,237},2).points(lh,le,"",{124,58,237},4).line(lh,guide,"Slope 2 guide",{148,163,184},1.5).title("Original reaction refinement").x_label("log10 h").y_label("log10 sampled nodal error");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(field).add(convergence).size(1100,550).title("Whole-field error and original nodal refinement checks").fit_panels().save_svg("build/fem_interval.svg");
}
